        "title": "Air pollution: Personal interventions and risk communication",
        "type": "url_citation",
        "url": "https://www.who.int/news-room/questions-and-answers/item/air-pollution-personal-interventions-and-risk-communication?utm_source=openai"
       }
      ],
      "text": "**KL’s latest reading I found is API 164 (unhealthy), updated at 4 p.m. on Saturday, October 17.** If your children have school today, I’d decide based on their health and how much time they’d spend outside—not just whether the school is officially closed.\n\nMalaysia’s Education Ministry says schools close at API 200, outdoor activities stop above 100, and parents may keep children home if they’re concerned, as long as they notify the school. ([jerebumalaysia.com](https://jerebumalaysia.com/en/school-closures/))\n\n**I’d lean toward keeping them home** if either child has asthma or another respiratory condition, is already coughing or wheezing, or would have a long outdoor commute. For otherwise healthy children, attending may be reasonable if the school confirms they’ll stay indoors; skip outdoor sports and assemblies, and minimize time outside. Children and people with respiratory conditions are more vulnerable to air pollution. ([who.int](https://www.who.int/news-room/questions-and-answers/item/air-pollution-personal-interventions-and-risk-communication?utm_source=openai))\n\nCheck your nearest DOE/APIMS reading and message the school before deciding, since conditions can vary by area and change through the day.",
